efca043c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75723" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1$ for $n>1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75723/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43f839b4-2b0e-5ab5-809a-741747f1016d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75723"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-49670"] :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-75727/selec