n}^{2}-1}$ for $n>1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{20\\operatorname{log}⁡n}{{n}^{2}-1}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{20\\operatorname{log}⁡n}{{n}^{2}-1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75952/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75952"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75963/selection/9ab64b945e95c1c3798cea70f4ae7ad55133b01b9b894848e70b56c91ffbae97" :answer/id #uuid "73ad8132-b34b-5289-b7a8-a229384353bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75963/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "98e2c887-77f6-5379-b383-be2744e43057" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75963/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "71cad2b9-3984-5d04-bc43-d4893766a39b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75963/selection/f5ba9cb7e3373c690e498bc176135d7b1a2093e80dfdec0ef9d7cefcc7228be5" :answer/id #uuid "73672372-939f-5dc2-8c3c-2b86613a09ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75963/selec